/**
@page exa_toss_ruler Planar toss ruler with time DG

@section toss_model Model and literature relationship

This example adapts the free-flight toss-ruler problem discussed by D. Kuhl and
E. Ramm, "Generalized Energy–Momentum Method for non-linear adaptive shell
dynamics", CMAME 178 (1999), 343–366,
https://doi.org/10.1016/S0045-7825(99)00024-9 .
The publisher's accessible abstract identifies the three-dimensional shell
benchmark. Geometry, material and pulse values were checked against the open
reproduction in C. S. Jog and P. Motamarri, J. Mechanics of Materials and
Structures 4 (2009), Section 4.6, Figure 21:
https://msp.org/jomms/2009/4-1/jomms-v4-n1-p09-p.pdf .

The reference domain is [0,0.3] x [-0.03,0.03] meters. Its out-of-plane
thickness is b=0.002 m, E=2.06e11 Pa, nu=0, and reference density is
7800 kg/m^3. Mass is 0.2808 kg. The L x W planform is represented by
biquadratic plane-stress continuum elements with finite-deformation St. Venant–
Kirchhoff elasticity. Thickness multiplies bulk, boundary and inertia terms
consistently. The fitted HMR/XTK mesh has one active material and no ghost
stabilization, supports, contact, damping material or artificial springs.
Initial displacement and reconstructed velocity are zero.

This is a planar adaptation, not a numerical reproduction of the shell curves.
In-plane bending and rotation replace the shell's three-dimensional bending,
torsion and rotation. In particular, the bending stiffness differs from the
weak-axis shell response. No three-dimensional loading pattern is projected
and claimed to be equivalent. The following planar loading is defined explicitly.

@section toss_loading Pulse and free flight

Let R(t)=40000*max(0,1-abs(2*t/0.004-1)) N/m. Nominal dead tractions on the
reference end faces are
 t_left=(0,R(t)/0.06), t_right=(0,-0.5*R(t)/0.06) Pa.
The two end forces are (0,b*R(t)) and (0,-0.5*b*R(t)) N, respectively.
All other faces are traction-free. The asymmetric pulse imparts linear and
angular momentum and excites elastic deformation. At 4 ms it vanishes and the
beam continues in unloaded motion, without gravity. This load is fixed in the
spatial coordinate directions and is integrated over reference boundary area.

The exact net impulse is (0,0.08) N*s. Once the pulse has ended,
 P_y=0.08, P_x=0,
 C_x=0.15, C_y=(0.08/0.2808)*(t-0.002) m.
These independent continuum balance checks are used for both process counts.
Angular momentum about the origin has a deformation-dependent applied torque
during loading; it is not approximated by a constant reference lever arm.

Time integration uses the new energy-corrected DG(1), alpha=4 formulation,
not the paper's generalized energy–momentum integrator. Each slab has two
independent displacement traces. The spatial forces use the DG weak and
incoming collocation tests; consistent mass and committed cluster velocity
history supply the inertial coupling. Amesos solves the nonsymmetric Newton
systems. Required settings are in the input's SOL parameter lists.

@section toss_running Configurations and output

The standalone Toss_Ruler.cpp input defaults to 30x4 Q2 elements, 800 uniform
slabs of 50 microseconds and T=0.04 s. Configuration globals have standalone
defaults and are declared extern when the EXA driver supplies them. The shared
EXA main handles initialization. The normal regression case uses 12x2 elements,
240 slabs at the same time step and T=0.012 s, covering the pulse and 8 ms of
unloaded motion. Each CTest case has a separate working directory.

Build and run the registered one-/two-rank regression cases:
@verbatim
cmake --build build_dbg --target Main_Toss_Ruler --parallel 4
ctest --test-dir build_dbg -R '^Toss_Ruler-' --output-on-failure
@endverbatim

For the longer demonstration, change to
build_dbg/projects/EXA/structure/dynamics/Toss_Ruler/runs/full and run:
@verbatim
mpiexec -n 2 ../../bin/Main_Toss_Ruler.exe '[.TossRulerFull]'
@endverbatim

Toss_Ruler.exo (with normal rank suffixes in parallel) stores every slab,
including outgoing UX/UY and IncomingUX/IncomingUY. In ParaView, form the vector
(UX,UY,0) with Calculator and use Warp By Vector with scale factor one. The
mesh is the reference configuration; animate the displacement, not the
undeformed mesh. FinalArea is a spatial endpoint IQI, equal to 0.018 m^2.

The EXA driver also writes Toss_Ruler_history.csv with time, center of mass,
linear momentum, angular momentum about the fixed origin, kinetic energy,
strain energy, total energy, minimum det(F), and the reference right-midpoint
node's outgoing displacement. No visualization exaggeration enters these
quantities. The standalone library alone writes Exodus; CSV generation belongs
to the EXA checker and runs in both explicit demonstration and regression cases.

Velocity at every node is reconstructed from the exported traces:
 v_n^- = (-3*q_(n-1)^+ + q_n^- + 2*q_(n-1)^-)/dt.
Here q_(n-1)^+ is the incoming trace on the current slab, q_n^- its outgoing
trace, and q_(n-1)^- the preceding outgoing trace (zero initially). This is
the same algebra as the committed velocity history, not gradt(displacement).
The checker gathers/averages duplicate VIS vertices across MPI ranks, then
uses MORIS Q2 interpolation and 5x5 spatial quadrature over reference cells to
compute the diagnostics. StVK energy is evaluated with the same plane-stress
law and nu=0 as the active CM.

@section toss_checks Verification scope

check_results verifies mesh dimension, reference coordinates, output times,
final area, finite fields, positive det(F), nonzero toss motion, impulse,
center-of-mass motion and linear momentum after release. gPrintReferenceValues
prints local Exodus mesh counts and final numerical fields for inspecting
reference data. Fixed 12 ms references were recorded from the verified serial
12x2 mesh and are reused on two ranks: tip displacement
(-0.002021374514632963,-0.02169343580232468) m, total energy
0.3072041374185144 J, and origin angular momentum -0.02399696451822027 N*m*s.
The checker uses relative tolerance 1e-6 (tip also has a 2e-9 m absolute
allowance). Local mesh counts are 125 nodes/24 elements on one rank and
65 nodes/12 elements on each of two ranks; those are not global parallel totals.
The full flight uses continuum balance and finite-field checks rather than
these shorter/coarser references. Momentum/energy histories are diagnostics for this dissipative
DG scheme. The final energy may not exceed release energy by more than 1e-5
relative; exact angular-momentum or energy conservation is not asserted. Literature shell response curves are not used as planar
regression references. The mesh/time settings in the driver define fixed
regression and demonstration cases; neither uses environment-variable tuning.

Validation on 2026-11-22: the 12 ms regression passed on one and two ranks,
and the 30x4-element, 800-slab 40 ms flight passed on two ranks. In that full
flight, final tip displacement was approximately (-0.0283133534,-0.0768799175) m,
P_y=0.079999999997 N*s, total energy=0.307151539 J, and energy decreased by
0.02203 percent from release to the end. These are observations for this
planar DG discretization, not the paper's three-dimensional shell references.
*/
